# Debug Mode: So the AI Agent Can See Its Output

**Goal:** When the debug agent instruments code, it should see its own logging. Prefer **server-side** instrumentation so the standard `fetch(...)` debug template works and output reaches the agent.

## Server-side: Use fetch (agent sees output)

**Server-side code** (API routes in `app/api/`, middleware, server components) runs in Node.js — no CSP. Use the standard debug `fetch(...)` one-liner; logs reach the NDJSON file and the agent can see them.

## Client-side: Use console.warn (CSP blocks fetch)

This project enforces a strict CSP in `middleware.ts`. `connect-src` allows only `'self'` and production domains (AWS, Cognito, Google, etc.). Any `fetch()` to the debug server (`http://127.0.0.1:*`) from **client code** (React components, hooks, browser JS) is blocked — logs never reach the agent.

Use `console.warn` instead:

```typescript
// #region agent log
console.warn('[DEBUG-<sessionId>] <hypothesis>', { key: value });
// #endregion
```

Ask the user to filter the browser DevTools console by `DEBUG-<sessionId>` so they can share or inspect client-side debug output. Do not relax CSP in `middleware.ts` just for debug instrumentation.

## Key file

- CSP: `middleware.ts` → `connect-src` directive (around line 92).
